Can you spot the critical bug in this javascript code?

```js
function reloadResource(){
    fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
        method: 'GET',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        setInterval(reloadResource, 1000);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}
```

The critical bug is in the `.then()` chain.

When you call `.then(response => response.json())`, the `.then()` method expects the **first** argument to be a **Promise**. However, `response` is a **Promise** (returned by `fetch()`), not a Promise itself.

This causes the `.then()` call to fail with a "TypeError: Unexpected type" error.

### How to fix it

You need to wrap the `.then()` call in a `Promise.all` or `Promise.resolve` to chain the results together.

Here is the corrected code:

```js
function reloadResource(){
    fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
        method: 'GET',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
    })
    .then(response => {
        // Wrap the response in a Promise so we can chain it
        return response.json();
    })
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        setInterval(reloadResource, 1000);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}
```

### Alternative (Simpler)
If you don't need to chain the results explicitly, you can use `Promise.all`:

```js
function reloadResource(){
    fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
        method: 'GET',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        setInterval(reloadResource, 1000);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}
```

