Here’s a simple, clear way to implement binary search in Java:

```java
public class BinarySearch {

    // Example array
    private static final int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90};

    // Function to search for a number
    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                return mid;
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        // Not found
        return -1;
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int target = 40;
        int result = binarySearch(arr, target);

        if (result != -1) {
            System.out.println("Found at index " + result);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Not found");
        }
    }
}
```

Key points:
- `left` and `right` start at the array’s beginning and end.
- `mid = left + (right - left) / 2` gives the middle index.
- If `arr[mid] == target`, return `mid`.
- If `arr[mid] < target`, search the right half (`left = mid + 1`).
- If `arr[mid] > target`, search the left half (`right = mid - 1`).
- If the loop finishes without finding the target, return `-1`.

